Third-party attribution
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Roast is an independent implementation of the SRT protocol, written against the
IETF draft "The SRT Protocol" (draft-sharabayko-srt) as the normative source. Two
existing implementations were used as references during development, and are
acknowledged here.

gosrt — https://github.com/datarhei/gosrt
    MIT License, Copyright (c) 2020-2022 FOSS GmbH

    Used as the primary structural reference. Several algorithms in Roast are
    ports of gosrt's own: the two-boundary ACK/delivery model in ReceiveBuffer,
    the send- and receive-side rate estimators, the RTT smoothing in
    SrtConnection, and the 16th/17th-packet bandwidth probe. Some tests are
    ported from gosrt's test suite, including its TestIssue67 regression case,
    and some are verified against golden vectors taken from it. The Java is
    written fresh rather than transliterated, but the debt is real and the MIT
    licence's attribution requirement is met here. Individual classes name what
    they took in their own javadoc.

libsrt — https://github.com/Haivision/srt
    Mozilla Public License 2.0, Copyright (c) 2018 Haivision Systems Inc.

    Used as the interoperability target and as the authority on behaviour where
    the draft is ambiguous — the keepalive and peer-idle-timeout model, MTU
    negotiation, the encryption key-material exchange, and the drift-correction
    formula were all settled by reading it. No libsrt code is copied into Roast,
    and none of Roast is a derivative work of it under the MPL: the source was
    read to learn observable protocol behaviour, which was then implemented
    independently in Java.
